  "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 4\n    n_machines = 4\n    processing_times = [[19, 21, 25, 25], [30, 10, 28, 11], [6, 12, 7, 27], [26, 20, 21, 22]]\n    machine_assignments = [[3, 2, 1, 0], [3, 0, 1, 2], [1, 0, 3, 2], [0, 1, 2, 3]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
  "LLM_description": "To: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Production Scheduling Challenge \u2013 Coordinating Jobs Across Specialized Workstations\nDate: [Today\u2019s Date]\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized machines (workstations) that support a variety of make-to-order products. Each customer order (job) moves through a defined series of operations, with each step performed on a specific machine for a known duration. The production mix requires careful coordination to ensure smooth flow and efficient use of shared resources.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nWe need to build a detailed production schedule that assigns start times and sequences for all operations across our machines. The complexity arises because:\n- Multiple jobs compete for the same machines at different times.\n- Each job\u2019s operations must follow a fixed sequence.\n- Machines can execute only one operation at a time, so conflicts must be avoided.\n- Starting an operation too early or late can cause cascading delays, idle time, or bottlenecks.\n\nOperating Requirements\n- Fixed job sequences: For every job, operations must be executed in the specified order; no downstream step can begin until the preceding step is finished.\n- Specific machine assignments and known durations: Each operation is tied to a particular machine and has a defined processing time.\n- Machine exclusivity: A machine can handle only one operation at any given time; there is no parallel processing on a single machine.\n- No interruptions: Once an operation starts on its assigned machine, it runs to completion without pause.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a feasible, conflict-free schedule that sequences all operations on their required machines and completes all jobs in the shortest overall time (minimize the makespan). In doing so, we aim to reduce machine idle time, prevent bottlenecks, and improve delivery predictability while adhering to the rules above.\n\nExpected Outcome\nA clear production plan that:\n- Specifies the order of operations on each machine.\n- Respects the sequence of operations within each job.\n- Allocates machine time without overlaps or interruptions.\n- Achieves the shortest possible total completion time for the full set of jobs.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 4\n- Number of machines (workstations): 4\n- Job-by-job production schedule (operations must be performed in the listed sequence):\n  Job 4: Op1 on Machine 0 for 26 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 1 for 20 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 2 for 21 time units \u2192 Op4 on Machine 3 for 22 time units\n  Job 1: Op1 on Machine 3 for 19 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 21 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 25 time units \u2192 Op4 on Machine 0 for 25 time units\n  Job 3: Op1 on Machine 1 for 6 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 12 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 3 for 7 time units \u2192 Op4 on Machine 2 for 27 time units\n  Job 2: Op1 on Machine 3 for 30 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 10 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 28 time units \u2192 Op4 on Machine 2 for 11 time units",
  "LLM_description_template": "To: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Production Scheduling Challenge \u2013 Coordinating Jobs Across Specialized Workstations\nDate: [Today\u2019s Date]\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized machines (workstations) that support a variety of make-to-order products. Each customer order (job) moves through a defined series of operations, with each step performed on a specific machine for a known duration. The production mix requires careful coordination to ensure smooth flow and efficient use of shared resources.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nWe need to build a detailed production schedule that assigns start times and sequences for all operations across our machines. The complexity arises because:\n- Multiple jobs compete for the same machines at different times.\n- Each job\u2019s operations must follow a fixed sequence.\n- Machines can execute only one operation at a time, so conflicts must be avoided.\n- Starting an operation too early or late can cause cascading delays, idle time, or bottlenecks.\n\nOperating Requirements\n- Fixed job sequences: For every job, operations must be executed in the specified order; no downstream step can begin until the preceding step is finished.\n- Specific machine assignments and known durations: Each operation is tied to a particular machine and has a defined processing time.\n- Machine exclusivity: A machine can handle only one operation at any given time; there is no parallel processing on a single machine.\n- No interruptions: Once an operation starts on its assigned machine, it runs to completion without pause.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a feasible, conflict-free schedule that sequences all operations on their required machines and completes all jobs in the shortest overall time (minimize the makespan). In doing so, we aim to reduce machine idle time, prevent bottlenecks, and improve delivery predictability while adhering to the rules above.\n\nExpected Outcome\nA clear production plan that:\n- Specifies the order of operations on each machine.\n- Respects the sequence of operations within each job.\n- Allocates machine time without overlaps or interruptions.\n- Achieves the shortest possible total completion time for the full set of jobs.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Machine assignments by job-operation: {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}\n- Processing times by job-operation: {PROCESSING_TIMES}"
